################ astral-sh/ty#3162
Unreachable names leak into lazy scopes as `Never` | OPEN  | created 2026-03-27 closed - | labels: bug,control flow,unreachable-code | author MeGaGiGaGon
--- body
### Summary

Fairly confusing to get the `invalid-type-form` instead of a `unresolved-reference` error.
[Playground](https://play.ty.dev/d9dcbcac-3cf4-4685-ab07-a46a146c1acd)
```py
from typing import TypeAlias

if False:
    ShouldBeUndefined = 1

reveal_type(ShouldBeUndefined)  # Name `ShouldBeUndefined` used when not defined (unresolved-reference)  # Revealed type: `Unknown` (revealed-type)

type A = ShouldBeUndefined  # Variable of type `Never` is not allowed in a type expression (invalid-type-form)
def foo(x: ShouldBeUndefined): ...  # Variable of type `Never` is not allowed in a type expression (invalid-type-form)
x: ShouldBeUndefined  # Variable of type `Never` is not allowed in a type expression (invalid-type-form)
```

If you switch to <=3.13 where annotations are not lazy by default, the bottom two become the expected `unresolved-reference` error.

### Version

playground 6e76b4cd8
--- comments (1)
[carljm 2026-04-22] This error can also show up in unreachable code. For example (reported in Discord):

```py
import multiprocessing.connection
from typing import TypeAlias

if hasattr(multiprocessing.connection, "PipeConnection"):
    Connection: TypeAlias = (
        multiprocessing.connection.Connection | multiprocessing.connection.PipeConnection
    )
else:
    Connection: TypeAlias = multiprocessing.connection.Connection
```

Emits "variable of type Never is not allowed in a type alias value", on one of the two branches depending whether platform is set to "win32" or linux/darwin.
################ python/mypy#9932
Type annotation "List"[int] generates runtime error but is accepted by mypy | OPEN  | created 2021-01-20 closed - | labels: feature,priority-2-low,topic-quoted-annotations | author erictraut
--- body
This bug was just logged against pyright (and fixed): https://github.com/microsoft/pyright/issues/1393

I noticed that mypy also fails to report this error.

```python
from typing import List
foo: "List"[int] = [1] # Accepted by mypy but generates runtime error
```
--- comments (4)
[gvanrossum 2021-01-20] FWIW in Python 3.10 this won't be a runtime error any more, so I suppose it's not a high priority.
~~
[erictraut 2021-01-20] Ah, good to know. I'll change the error check to be conditional on the Python version.
~~
[hauntsaninja 2021-01-22] In general, mypy unfortunately doesn't keep track of whether or not a type is quoted / would be evaluated at runtime (we added some support for checking this for PEP 604 syntax use, but that's about it). Linking https://github.com/python/mypy/issues/948
~~
[JelleZijlstra 2022-03-19] I think Guido was referring to PEP 563 / `from __future__ import annotations`, which didn't end up being turned on by default in 3.10, so this is a runtime error in 3.10 too.
################ python/mypy#21747
`closed=True` should not be supported for Python <3.15 | OPEN  | created 2026-07-18 closed - | labels: bug,topic-typed-dict | author A5rocks
--- body
<!--
If you're not sure whether what you're experiencing is a mypy bug, please see the "Question and Help" form instead.
Please consider:

- checking our common issues page: https://mypy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/common_issues.html
- searching our issue tracker: https://github.com/python/mypy/issues to see if it's already been reported
- asking on gitter chat: https://gitter.im/python/typing
-->

**Bug Report**

<!--
If you're reporting a problem with a specific library function, the typeshed tracker is better suited for this report: https://github.com/python/typeshed/issues

If the project you encountered the issue in is open source, please provide a link to the project.
-->

mypy accepts `closed=True` on TypedDicts before Python 3.15. I think this should be allowed in typing-only files, but not at runtime.

**To Reproduce**

```python
from typing import TypedDict

class A(TypedDict, closed=True):
    x: int

a: A = {"x": 5, "y": "5"}  # E
```

**Expected Behavior**

An error when defining `A`.

**Actual Behavior**

No error when defining `A`.

**Your Environment**

<!-- Include as many relevant details about the environment you experienced the bug in -->

Reproduced in mypy-play.
- Mypy version used: 2.3.0
- Mypy command-line flags: N/A
- Mypy configuration options from `mypy.ini` (and other config files): N/A
- Python version used: 3.12

<!-- You can freely edit this text, please remove all the lines you believe are unnecessary. -->

